// oob_array_read — Array read out of bounds (requires bounds-check IR, always on in VUMA 2.0)
// Expected exit code: 134
// Compile: compile_dump <file> <out> <backend> --opt-level=O3
//
// Bounds SAFE-ONLY negative test: allocate a 4-byte buffer
// (`[u8; 4]`) and read past its end at index 10. With bounds-check IR
// (always on in VUMA 2.0), the injected `UGe(idx, 4)` bounds check
// evaluates to true (10 >= 4) and dispatches to `__oob_trap`, which
// aborts with exit code 134 (SIGABRT, 128 + 6).
//
// IMPORTANT: This test is NOT in the regular `bounds_wave1/` gold
// standard suite because it only passes with bounds-check IR injected
// (always on in VUMA 2.0). Without bounds-check IR (legacy mode), the
// OOB access is undefined behaviour: the process may crash (SIGSEGV →
// exit 139), may silently read garbage, or may even return a value.
// The expected exit code below assumes bounds-check IR is injected.
//
// VUMA Key Concepts:
//   - state_new(Layout) allocation (PMT idiom)
//   - `[u8; 4]` array field with a known 4-byte size
//   - out-of-bounds Load (read) at index 10 — index is a runtime
//     variable so O3 cannot trivially DCE the access; the injected
//     `UGe(idx, 4)` check folds to true and the trap branch survives
//     DCE because it is statically reachable.
//   - exit 134 == SIGABRT (128 + 6) emitted by `__oob_trap`.

layout Buf = { data: [u8; 4] }

transform main() -> i32 {
    let b = state_new(Buf);
    // Read past the 4-byte array — should trap with exit 134
    let idx = 10;
    return b.data[idx];
}
